# API Contract Framework Decision Round Table

## Protocol
Agents should write to this doc only when instructed to. Different agents are likely to come in with different perspectives and it is valuable to collect them all in one place, but we do not want a recursive environment of agents responding to each other unprompted. If you are an agent and this is your first time reading this round table document your role, unless the prompt that brought you here specified otherwise, is to read the proposal and provide any questions or feedback in the space reserved for your persona. You should avoid reading other agents responses at first and provide your own take. Feel free to make arguements for or against the proposal, provide any new details you find worth considering, or ask any questions that the proposal leaves unclear. If you've already been here before then you are likely being asked to provide a follow up. Leave your comment in the follow up section

## Proposal for new underlying api contract framework
We have found ourself in a position where the database, server code and comments, wiki, test plan and tests, bruno files, and mcp files have their own lever of 'stating what the truth is' and there is a high effort of maintenance to keep everything in agreement. The goal of this story is to implement a framework that automates the agreements as much as makes sense, with the understanding that some level of hand maintenance is unavoidable.

The proposal is:
1. The wiki remains behavior driven and is not concerned with specifying things like data architecture or api contracts.
2. The specifics of api contracts can instead be auto-generated in OpenApi-style documentation
3. Test plans start with the wiki to determine the relevant test cases but may be aware of the contracts to determine the specific expectations that prove the rule. Some tests cases can automatically be mapped as "handled by the framework" if we land on a framework that automatically tests/enforces certain rules
4. The framework should be able to determine a single source of truth that can align several things, but ultimately Bruno and the MCP will likely require hand maintenance regardless of the framework we decide on
5. New check scripts may be a viable way to further help highlight where maintenance is needed and, as is the current process, Drift Audits are a final check to give confidence that the pieces remain in alignment. Nothing will completely eliminate the need for this
6. Three framework options were considered and evaluated in docs\planning\tech-debt-stories\api-contract-framework-spike.md
7. One proposed option - **Zod 4 native conversion plus small OpenAPI 3.1 assembly and generated client declarations** - was piloted and is the currently recommended solution to move forward with. The review can be found here: docs\planning\tech-debt-stories\api-contract-framework-spike-review.md

Please read the initial spike, the final review, and any additional supporting material you feel is pertinent and leave any comments you have about the proposal. The review has some 'Questions for later role input' addressed to most of you for your consideration. Please provide your perspective if you believe the review analysis has not considered how the decision effects your role or if you just have new questions or comments to add. Let us know if you are in consensus with recommendation or if you have reservations and would like to give more consideration to another option.

*note: In this round table - unless clearly specified otherwise - 'Architect' is a separate instance from the arcitect that implemented the pilot and review. The purpose is to get a fresh perspective from each role.*

- Nathan

